% Quoting and escaping. Getting any of this wrong is an injection, not a
% formatting nit, which is why it is the first file.

### name: lex.number.canonical-form-survives
--- note
2.50 keeps its scale: scale is part of a SEL number and part of what SQL
DECIMAL arithmetic reads. Reformatting it would change the value.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
2.50
--- expect
2.50
===
### name: lex.number.leading-zeros-canonicalised-by-the-parser
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
007
--- expect
7
===
### name: lex.number.negative-is-an-operator-not-a-literal
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
-5
--- expect
(-5)
===
### name: lex.text.simple
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"hello"
--- expect
'hello'
===
### name: lex.text.single-quote-doubles
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"it's"
--- expect
'it''s'
===
### name: lex.text.backslash-doubles-on-mysql-family
--- note
MariaDB treats \ as an escape inside a string literal under the default
sql_mode. PostgreSQL and SQLite do not, and their maps omit the rule.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
'a\b'
--- expect
'a\\b'
===
### name: lex.text.empty
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
""
--- expect
''
===
