### name: lex.ident.table-and-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"TOTAL": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
TOTAL
--- expect
`o`.`total`
===
### name: lex.ident.no-table
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"TOTAL": {"kind": "column", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
TOTAL
--- expect
`total`
===
### name: lex.ident.quote-inside-a-name-is-escaped
--- note
A backtick in a column name must not end the identifier early.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ODD": {"kind": "column", "table": "a`b", "column": "c`d", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
ODD
--- expect
`a``b`.`c``d`
===
### name: lex.ident.raw-bypasses-quoting
--- note
The one place a caller can inject arbitrary SQL, spelled distinctly so it is
visible in a review.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"NOW": {"kind": "column", "raw": "CURRENT_TIMESTAMP", "type": "UNKNOWN"}}
--- source
NOW
--- expect
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
### name: lex.number.long-numeral-reaches-sql
--- note
The SQL layer parses numerals too -- Emit calls Dec::parse and re-renders
Dec::format's output rather than the caller's characters -- so a host limit on
numeral length is not an evaluator-only concern. On the Python host this program
raised a ValueError out of compile(), before the translator ran at all, while
PHP produced the SQL. spec/SPEC.md 6.4 sets the length a numeral may have; both
hosts must reach that answer, and neither may reach it by crashing.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source

--- expect

===
### name: lex.text.non-bmp-literal-is-passed-through
--- note
A character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane -- four UTF-8 bytes, one SEL
character -- reaches the emitted SQL as itself. The translator does not escape
it, which is right: the map's job is a byte-preserving literal and the
connection's job is a charset that can carry it.

That second half is the caller's, and it is the same edge the first external
user of this layer met from the other side. `utf8mb4_bin` here is a four-byte
collation, but a connection negotiated as `utf8mb3` cannot carry the character
at all and MariaDB will replace or reject it. Declaring a dialect with a
different textCollate does not fix that; the CONNECTION charset does. Nothing
in the layer can check it, so the layer says what it emits and stops.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"😀" $== "x"
--- expect
(CAST('😀' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('x' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: lex.text.non-bmp-literal-postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
"😀" $== "x"
--- expect
(CAST('😀' AS TEXT) COLLATE "C" = CAST('x' AS TEXT) COLLATE "C")
===
### name: lex.text.non-bmp-literal-sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"😀" $== "x"
--- expect
(CAST('😀' AS TEXT) = CAST('x' AS TEXT))
===
### name: lex.text.non-bmp-length-is-one-character
--- note
And the reason the literal above is one character and not two: SEL counts code
points, so CHAR_LENGTH is the right server-side spelling and LENGTH -- bytes on
MariaDB -- would answer 4.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEN("😀") == 1
--- expect
(CHAR_LENGTH('😀') = 1)
===
